; 11-save.kurt

; `save` keeps what you did: it writes the lines that were accepted so far
; to a file. In the shell, that is your session without the lines that
; failed, and without the ones that only showed something (`theory`,
; `help`, ...) -- so you can try things out, and then keep what worked.
; In a file like this one, it is simply its lines so far.

bool A, B
use A implies B "modus-ponens"
use A

show B
proof
    A
    B
qed

save "/tmp/kurt-tutorial-save-demo.kurt"
; the path is relative to the current working directory, not to this file

; The saved file is ordinary Kurt source: checking it proves `B` again (and
; writes its certificates to a `.kurtc` next to it, see doc/kurt-doc.md).
; Try it from the command line:
;
;     kurt /tmp/kurt-tutorial-save-demo.kurt
;
; `save` needs all blocks closed and no pending `show` -- otherwise the saved
; file wouldn't load.
